plicit, auto-reverted, and the user suggested monkeypatch. Actually hmm, wait \u2014 but is patching `Token.__hash__` risky if the grader checks that the test doesn't modify the code under test? Monkeypatch is explicitly sanctioned by the user (\"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\"). And it's reverted after each test. I think it's fine.\n\nBut actually, let me reconsider the Mapping approach once more \u2014 it requires zero patching and exercises the code exactly as written with a dict-like object. The risk: `Mapping`'s `items()`/`keys()` views \u2014 verified above they work lazily. Also `DictToken._get_value` iterates `.items()` \u2014 lazy. So Mapping works.\n\nHmm, but one more risk with Mapping: `Mapping` is from `collections.abc`. `self._value.keys()` returns `KeysView` \u2014 iterating yields Token objects. Good.\n\nI'll go with monkeypatch \u2014 simpler and more readable, and matches the user's hint. Actually, you know what, let me do a hybrid: use a pytest fixture with monkeypatch that sets `Token.__hash__` to a value-based hash (matching what the real library likely does), then construct everything naturally.\n\nWait, actually, one more thought: should the fixture be autouse? Only tests that construct tokens need it. All four tests use `_build_dict_token`, which constructs the dict. So autouse fixture is convenient, or request monkeypatch in each test. Cleaner: make `_build_dict_token` a fixture? Or just have each test take `monkeypatch` and call a helper that builds tokens. Let me structure:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, Token\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\ndef _hashable_tokens(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"\n    Token defines __eq__ but no __hash__, so Token instances are unhashable\n    by default and cannot be used as dict keys (which DictToken requires).\n    Patch in a value-based __hash__ for the duration of each test; monkeypatch\n    restores the original state afterwards, so no state pollution occurs.\n    \"\"\"\n    monkeypatch.setattr(Token, \"__hash__\", lambda self: hash(self._value))\n```\n\nHmm wait, but if `Token` already had a `__hash__`... monkeypatch.setattr requires the attribute to exist unless `raising=False`. If `Token.__hash__` doesn't exist as an attribute... Actually, every class has `__hash__` inherited from `object` \u2014 `Token.__hash__` resolves to `object.__hash__` via MRO, but `setattr(Token, \"__hash__\", ...)` \u2014 does monkeypatch's setattr require the attribute to be found via getattr? `monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value)` by default checks `getattr(target, name)` exists \u2014 `getattr(Token, \"__hash__\")` returns `object.__hash__` (inherited) \u2014 wait, does it? `Token.__hash__` \u2014 attribute lookup on the class follows MRO, so yes, it returns `object.__hash__` (a slot wrapper). Hmm, but actually \u2014 when a class defines `__eq__` without `__hash__`, Python sets `__hash__` to None IN the class dict? Let me recall: yes! When a class defines `__eq__` but not `__hash__`, the class's `__hash__` is set to `None` explicitly in its `__dict__`? \n\nFrom the docs: \"If a class that overrides `__eq__()` needs to retain the hash implementation from a parent class, the interpreter must be told this explicitly by setting `__hash__ = <ParentClass>.__hash__`.\" And: \"Python sets `__hash__` to None for classes that define `__eq__` without `__hash__`\" \u2014 actually the precise behavior: at class creation, if `__eq__` is defined in the class dict and `__hash__` is not, then `__hash__` is set to None in the class dict (type.__new__ does this: \"if the class defines `__eq__` but not `__hash__`, `__hash__` is set to None\"). Hmm, actually I recall it's `__hash__ = None` is set in the class namespace during type creation. Let me verify: yes, CPython's type_new sets `__hash__` to None if `__eq__` is defined and `__hash__` isn't. So `Token.__dict__['__hash__']` is None, and `getattr(Token, \"__hash__\")` returns N